ould drop Black's winning chances from 68% to 50% (0.00).","41…Rf6 wins a pawn, picking it up with 42…Rxh6, and threatens …Rxf4, winning the queen.","Sets up a sequence and wins a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 42…Rxh6.","It threatens …Rxf4, winning the queen, so White has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":95,"name":"48.Qf4+","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"48.Qf4+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The queen forks the king and the pawn: both can't be saved at once.","The queen joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the Black king.","The next best move, 48.h7, would drop White's winning chances from 73% to 53% (+0.32).","48.Qf4+ forks the king and the pawn.","Protects the knight on g4, which was hanging.","Black answers 48…Nd6."]},{"ply":98,"name":"49…Rxg4","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"49…Rxg4!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on g4 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: White's best reply is 50.Qxd6+ Kxd6 51.h8=Q Ke7 instead (the engine rates it 0.00).","Takes the knight on g4.","Moving away uncovers the bishop on e4, which now hits the h7 pawn.","49…Rxg4 wins the queen.","Takes the knight on g4 and wins the queen once the captures settle."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"49…Rxg4!! gives up the rook on g4 to stay in the game: after it Black holds the balance (0.00); anything else leaves Black losing (+6.13)."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"49…Rxg4 puts the rook on g4 where it can be taken by the queen on f4. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the knight on g4."},{"title":"If White takes the rook","text":"50.Qxg4 is met by 50…Bxh7, taking the h7 pawn. Then 51…Be4 threatens …Bxf3, winning the queen for a bishop, and 52…Kc6 threatens …Bxc2, winning a bishop. Black is a pawn down: Black holds the balance (−0.19)."},{"title":"White's best try","text":"White's best is to leave the rook alone and play 50.Qxd6+. Black answers 50…Kxd6, taking the queen on d6 and threatening …Bxh7, winning a bishop and a pawn. Then 51…Ke7 threatens …Rxg2+, winning a bishop and a pawn, and 53…Rxg2+ gives check. Black ends up two pawns ahead: Black holds the balance (0.00)."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 49…Bxc2, is clearly weaker (+6.13 against 0.00). White answers 50.Rxc2, taking the bishop on c2 and threatening Rxa2, winning the queen for a knight. That would leave Black losing."}]},{"ply":100,"name":"50…Bxh7","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"50…Bxh7! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the pawn on h7.","Leaves White with an isolated pawn on g2: a long-term weakness.","The next best move, 50…Bf5, would drop Black's winning chances from 52% to 7% (+7.06).","50…Bxh7 wins a pawn.","Takes the h7 pawn and wins a pawn once the captures settle.","White answers 51.Qf4, pinning the knight on d6 to the king."]},{"ply":135,"name":"68.Bxf5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"68.Bxf5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the pawn on f5.","The next best move, 68.Ba4, would drop White's winning chances from 89% to 65% (+1.70).","68.Bxf5 wins a pawn.","Takes the f5 pawn and wins a pawn once the captures settle.","Black answers 68…Nc7.","After 68…Nc7 69.Ka3 Ke7 70.Bxe6 Nxe6 71.Kb4, the bishops are traded and White comes out a pawn up (+4.48)."]},{"ply":143,"name":"72.Ka3","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"72.Ka3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The next best move, 72.Rg6, would drop White's winning chances from 85% to 70% (+2.24).","72.Ka3 prepares Kb4 and Ka5.","The engine's plan continues with Kb4 and Ka5: this move is the first step.","Black answers 72…Ne6.","Engine line: 72…Ne6 73.Kb4 Ke7 74.Ka5 Ng5 75.Rh5 (+4.19)."]}],"opening":{"name":"Torre Attack: 